ympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/526"} "Gewichtheberhalle, Messegelände (Halle 7), München"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/29531"} "22 from 18 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Total of best lifts in military press, snatch, and clean &amp; jerk determined placement.  Ties broken by lightest bodyweight."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/56500"} "Waldemar Baszanowski"] " was the gold medalist in 1964 and 1968 and was considered the greatest pound-for-pound lifter of the 60s. He was back but had lost the last two World Championships to his teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/56516"} "Zbigniew Kaczmarek"] ", although Baszanowski still held the world record with 450.0 kg. The lead after the press was taken by Bulgarian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55303"} "Mladen Kuchev"] ", who was unknown internationally, but set a world record with his lift of 157.5 kg. Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/56899"} "Mukharby Kirzhinov"] ", bronze medalist at the 1971 Worlds, then snatched 135.0 kg and he and Kuchev were tied going into the final lifts. When Kirzhinov cleaned &amp; jerked a world record of 177.5, he had the gold medal with a world record total of 460.0 kg, followed by Kuchev, who matched Baszanowski’s prior world record. Kaczmarek won bronze, with Baszanowski placing fourth. Austrian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55204"} "Walter Legel"] " originally placed 15th, but was disqualified for a doping positive, having been found with traces of amphetamines in his system."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/56899"} "Mukharby Kirzhinov"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/URS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-